## Polymer Acceptor with Optimal LUMO Energy Level for AllPolymer Solar Cells
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x www.rsc.org/
A key parameter for polymer electron acceptors is the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy level $(E_{\mathsf{L U M O}})$ . For the state-of-the-art polymer electron acceptors based on naphthalene diimide (NDI) unit, their $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ are low-lying and cannot be tuned, leading to low open-circuit voltage $(V_{\circ\mathsf{c}})$ of the resulting all-polymer solar cells (all-PSCs). We report that polymer electron acceptors based on double $B\leftarrow N$ bridged bipyridine (BNBP) unit exhibit tunable $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ because of their delocalized LUMOs over the polymer backbones. The $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of the copolymer of BNBP unit and selenophene unit (P-BNBPSe) is lower by $0.16\ \mathrm{eV}$ than that of the copolymer of BNBP unit and thiophene unit (P-BNBP-T). As a result, the energy levels of P-BNBP-Se match well with the widely-used polymer donor, poly[(ethylhexyl-thiophenyl)-benzodithiophene(ethylhexyl)-thienothiophene] (PTB7-Th). The electron mobility of P-BNBP-Se $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}}=2.07\times10^{-4}\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ is also higher than that of P-BNBP-T $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}}=7.16\times10^{-5}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ . While the all-PSC device based on the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T blend shows a moderate power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $2.27\%$ , the corresponding device with P-BNBP-Se as the acceptor exhibits a PCE as high as $4.26\%$ . Moreover, owing to the proper $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se, the all-PSC device of P-BNBP-Se shows a $V_{\infty}$ up to $\displaystyle1.03~\mathsf{V}.$ , which is higher by $0.22\mathrm{~V~}$ than that with conventional NDI-based polymer acceptor. These results indicate that BNBP-based polymers can give all-PSCs with high PCE, remarkably high $V_{\mathsf{o c}}$ and small photon energy loss.
## Introduction
All-polymer solar cells (all-PSCs), which utilize polymers as both electron donor and electron acceptor, have attracted much attention recently because of their great advantages over conventional polymer/fullerene PSCs.1 These advantages include enhanced light absorption of polymer acceptors, low cost, and improved mechanical/thermal stability. Great progress on all-PSCs has been made by using absorptioncomplementary polymer donor/acceptor, optimizing the blend morphology, or developing new polymer acceptors.2 However, further development of all-PSCs is severely limited by the lack of excellent polymer acceptors.3 To date, only several specific polymer acceptors based on naphthalene diimide (NDI) unit, perylenediimide (PDI) unit and $B\leftarrow N$ bridged thienylthiazole (BNTT) unit can work as polymer acceptors for efficient allPSCs with power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) exceeding 4%.4,5
A key parameter for polymer acceptors is the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy level $(E_{\mathsf{L U M O}})$ . In all-PSCs, the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ difference between acceptor and donor $(\Delta E_{\mathsf{L U M O}})$ is regarded as the driving force for charge separation.6 The difference between the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of acceptor and the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level $(E_{\mathsf{H O M O}})$ of donor is related to the open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of all-PSCs.6 Therefore, to give large $\Delta E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ for effective charge separation and to maximize $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of polymer acceptor must be carefully optimized. The state-of-the-art polymer acceptors are the NDI-based conjugated polymers.4 Unfortunately, the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of these polymers are fixed to be ca. $-3.85\mathrm{~eV}$ and cannot be effectively tuned, leading to low $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of the resulting all-PSCs. According to the study by Takimiya et al.,7 the fixed $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of NDI-based polymers are due to the localized LUMOs on the NDI units. The $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of NDI-based conjugated polymers are determined by the NDI unit and are not affected by the copolymerization units. Thus, it is important but challenging to develop polymer acceptors with tunable ELUMO.
Following our strategy to develop polymer acceptors using $B\leftarrow N$ unit,5 we have reported a new electron-deficient building block based on $B\leftarrow N$ unit, double $B\leftarrow N$ bridged bipyridine (BNBP), to develop polymer acceptor.8 In this manuscript, we report that BNBP-based polymer acceptors show tunable $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ because of their delocalized LUMOs over the polymer backbones. The $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of the copolymer of BNBP unit and selenophene unit (P-BNBP-Se) is lower by $0.16\mathrm{eV}$ than that of the copolymer of BNBP unit and thiophene unit (P-BNBP-T). As a result, the energy levels of P-BNBP-Se match well with the widely-used polymer donor, poly[(ethylhexyl-thiophenyl)- benzodithiophene-(ethylhexyl)-thienothiophene] (PTB7-Th).9 While the all-PSC device based on the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T blend shows a moderate PCE of $2.27\%$ , the corresponding device with P-BNBP-Se as the acceptor exhibits a PCE as high as $4.26\%$ with a remarkably high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $\boldsymbol{1.03\ \vee}$ These results indicate that BNBP-based polymer acceptors have different electronic structure from that of the classical NDI-based polymer acceptors and that they can give all-PSCs with remarkably high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and high PCE.
## Results and discussion
Scheme 1 shows the synthetic route of P-BNBP-Se and PBNBP-T. The three monomers were prepared following the literature methods and the two polymers were synthesized in a Stille-polymerization condition.8 Their chemical structures are confirmed by $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR and elemental analysis. According to gel permeation chromotography (GPC) with 1,2,4- trichlorobenzene as the eluent at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , the number-average molecular weight $(M_{\mathfrak{n}})$ and polydispersity (PDI) are $26.3~\mathsf{k D a}$ and 1.93 for P-BNBP-Se and $46.2\ \mathsf{k D a}$ and 1.81 for P-BNBP-T, respectively. According to thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), PBNBP-T and P-BNBP-Se show good thermal stability with thermal decomposition temperatures $(\overline{{T_{\mathrm{d}}}})$ of over $350^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (ESI). In addition, the two polymers show good solubility in common organic solvents, including chlorobenzene (CB), chloroform $(C H C l_{3})$ and $o$ -dichlorobenzene (o-DCB).
bithiophene))) (N2200 or P(NDI2ODT2)) (Fig. 1a).11 As shown in Fig. 2, the calculated LUMO of the model compound of N2200 is localized on the NDI units, indicating that its $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ is determined by the NDI unit and cannot be effectively tuned by changing the co-monomer units. This is consistent with the DFT calculation and experimental results of NDI-based conjugated polymers in literatures.4,11 In contrast, the calculated LUMOs of the model compounds of P-BNBP-Se/PBNBP-T are delocalized over the BNBP units and the selenophene/thiophene units. Therefore, the LUMO levels of BNBP-based polymers are determined by both the BNBP unit and the co-monomer unit. The LUMO levels of BNBP-based polymers should be effectively tuned by changing the comonomer units.
Cyclic voltammetry was employed to estimate the
LUMO/HOMO energy levels of the two polymers (ESI).12 As shown in Fig. 3a, P-BNBP-Se exhibits the irreversible reduction and oxidation waves with the onset potentials of $E_{\mathrm{onset}}\mathrm{^{red}=-}$ $\boldsymbol{1.14\mathrm{~V~}}$ and setox = +1.04 V, respectively. Accordingly, the E of P-BNBP-Se are estimated to be $-3.66~\mathrm{eV}/-5.84$ eV (Table 1). Similarly, the ELUMO/HOMO of P-BNBP-T are estimated to be –3.50 eV/–5.77 eV (Table 1). As reported previously, the model compound of BNBP unit itself has the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of $-3.19\ \mathsf{e V}.$ . The $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of the two BNBP-based polymers are much lower than that of the BNBP unit. Moreover, the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se is lower than that of P-BNBP-T by $0.16\mathrm{eV}$ These results confirm that the LUMO levels of BNBP-based polymers can be effectively tuned by changing the comonomer units. This is consistent with the delocalized LUMOs in DFT calculation results. The lower-lying $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se is attributed to the less electronegativity of Se atom (2.4) than S atom (2.5) and the empty orbital of Se atom.13
Fig. 3b shows the absorption spectra of P-BNBP-Se and PBNBP-T in dilute $o$ -DCB solution and in thin film. Both of the two polymers in solution show broad absorption bands around $\lambda=580~\mathsf{n m}$ . The absorption spectrum is slightly redshifted for P-BNBP-Se compared to P-BNBP-T. In thin film, P-BNBP-Se exhibits the maximum absorption at $635\ \mathsf{n m}$ , while P-BNBP-T shows the absorption peak at $622\ \mathsf{n m}$ . Both of the two films show the high absorption coefficients (ε), suggesting their intense light absorption. According to the onset absorption wavelength in thin films, the optical band gaps $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of P-BNBPSe and P-BNBP-T are estimated to be 1.87 eV and $1.92\ \mathsf{e V},$ , respectively. The electron mobilities $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}})$ of P-BNBP-Se and PBNBP-T were estimated using space-charge-limited current (SCLC) method with the current density-voltage curves of the electron-only devices (device structure: ITO/PEIE/polymer/Ca/Al).14 The electron mobility of P-BNBPSe $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}}=2.07\times10^{-4}\ c m^{2}\bigvee^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ is higher than that of P-BNBP-T $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}}=7.16\times10^{-5}\ c m^{2}\bigvee^{-1}{\mathsf{s}}^{-1})$ (ESI). The higher electron mobility of P-BNBP-Se is due to the stronger intermolecular interactions in Se-containing polymers because of the larger and more polarizable radii of selenium atom than sulfur atom. This is confirmed by the smaller $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance of PBNBP-Se $\begin{array}{r}{(d_{\pi-\pi}=3.77\mathring{\mathsf{A}}}\end{array}$ ) than that of P-BNBP-T $\left(d_{\pi-\pi}=3.81\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}\right)$ (ESI). The electron mobility of P-BNBP-Se is comparable to the hole mobilities of typical polymer electron donors, which is very favourable for the application as polymer electron acceptor in all-PSCs.
To investigate the application of P-BNBP-Se and P-BNBP-T as electron acceptors in all-PSCs, we select a widely-used polymer donor, PTB7-Th. All-PSC devices were fabricated with the configuration of ITO/PEDOT:PSS/PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-Se or PBNBP- $\pmb{\tau}/\mathsf{C a}/\mathsf{A l}$ (ESI). The active layer was spin-coated from the blend in $o$ -DCB solution without any additives. Fig. 4 shows the current density-voltage $\left(J-W\right)$ curves under AM 1.5G illumination $(100\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}})$ and external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the optimal devices. The photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 2. The PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T (3:1, w:w) device shows a PCE of $2.27\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $\begin{array}{r}{1.12\lor,}\end{array}$ , a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $5.24\ m\mathsf{A}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and a fill factor (FF) of 0.39. The device based on the PTB7-Th:P-BNBPSe (2:1, w:w) blend exhibits a PCE of $4.26\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.03 $\mathsf{v},$ a $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ of $10.02~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and a FF of 0.42. This PCE value is comparable to that of the reference all-PSC device based on the PTB7-Th:N2200 (1:1,w:w) blend from chloroform solution $(P C E=4.57\%)$ , indicating that P-BNBP-Se is an excellent polymer acceptor. Compared with the device of P-BNBP-T, the device of P-BNBP-Se shows slightly decreased $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and much increased $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ . The slightly decreased $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ is attributed to the lower $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se than that of P-BNBP-T. On the other hand, the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of the P-BNBP-Se device is higher than that of the N2200 device by 0.22 eV (Table 2) because the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of PBNBP-Se is higher than that of N2200. The much increased $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the P-BNBP-Se device than that of the P-BNBP-T device is in accordance with their EQE values $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\prime}\mathsf{E}\mathsf{Q}\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{m a x}}=0.47$ for P-BNBP-Se and $\mathsf{E Q E}_{\mathsf{m a x}}=\mathsf{0}.25$ for P-BNBP-T) (Fig. 4b). The $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ calculated from the integration of EQE spectra agree well with the $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ values obtained from the $\jmath{-}V$ scans within an error of $5\%$ .
Charge carrier mobilities of the two blends were investigated using SCLC method with the electron-only and hole-only devices (ESI).14 The electron mobility and hole mobility $(\mu_{\mathfrak{h}})$ of the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-Se blend are estimated to be $3.34~\times~10^{-5}~\mathsf{c m}^{2}~\mathsf{V}^{-1}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ and $2.38\times10^{-4}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1},$ , respectively. In comparison, the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T blend exhibits the $\mu_{\mathrm{e}}=5.96\times10^{-6}{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}{\mathsf{V}}^{-1}{\mathsf{s}}^{-1}$ and $\mu_{\mathsf{h}}=7.28\times10^{-4}{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}$ $\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1},$ , respectively. The higher electron mobility and the balanced electron/hole mobilities of the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-Se blend are due to the enhanced electron mobility of P-BNBP-Se. We also investigated the bimolecular charge recombination in the all-PSC devices by the light-intensity dependence of the $J-$ $V$ curves (Fig. 5). The $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ follows a power-law dependence on the illumination intensity $({J_{\mathrm{sc}}}^{\propto}{P_{\mathrm{light}}}^{\alpha}).$ , where $P_{\mathrm{light}}$ is light intensity and $\alpha$ is the calculated power-law exponent. The $\alpha$ value is 0.93 for the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-Se device and 0.94 for the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T device, which are close to unity, suggesting that bimolecular charge recombination is weak in the two devices at short circuit condition.15 Both the weak bimolecular recombination and the high and balanced electron/hole mobilities of the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-Se can explain its excellent device performance.
The morphologies of the PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-Se and PTB7-Th:PBNBP-T blends were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM). As shown in Fig. 6, their TEM images exhibit similar nano/microstructures without large-size aggregation. The AFM images of the two blends similarly reveal smooth surface morphologies with a same root-mean-square (RMS) roughness of $1.47\ \mathsf{n m}$ and the domain sizes of around $20{-}40~\mathsf{n m}$ . The phase separation morphologies of the two blends are beneficial for the good all-PSC devices.
In organic photovoltaics (OPVs), the $\Delta E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of donor and acceptor is regarded as the driving force for charge separation. The $\Delta E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ should be larger than a specific value for efficient charge separation. If $\Delta E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ is too large, there is much energy loss in the charge separation process, leading to low $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ because $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of OPVs is related to the difference between $E_{\mathsf{H O M O}}$ of donor and $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of acceptor.6 In our previous report, the allPSC device based on the PTB7:P-BNBP-T blend $(\varDelta E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}=0.19$ eV) shows a good PCE of $3.38\%$ .8 As shown in Figure 1b, the $\varDelta E_{\mathrm{{LUMO}}}$ is only 0.06 eV for PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T, and thus the allPSC device shows a high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ but produces a low PCE due to the insufficient charge separation.4g As the $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se is lower than that of P-BNBP-T, the $\varDelta E_{\mathrm{{LUMO}}}$ for PTB7-Th:P-BNBPSe is increased to be $0.22\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and ensure efficient charge separation, resulting in the higher $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ and PCE values. Moreover, due to the proper $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se, the PTB7- Th:P-BNBP-Se device produces a high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $\pmb{1.03}~\mathsf{V}_{\cdot}$ , which is higher than that of the PTB7-Th:N2200 device by $0.22\mathrm{~V~}$ . These results indicate that the proper $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se plays an important role on enhancing all-PSCs device performance.
It is worthy to note the remarkably low photon energy loss $(E_{\mathsf{l o s s}})$ of the all-PSCs based on P-BNBP-Se and P-BNBP-T. $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ is defined as the difference between the lowest optical bandgap of donor/acceptor and the $e V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of the organic photovoltaic (OPV) device $(E_{\mathrm{loss}}=E_{\mathrm{g}}–e V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ .16 Typically, OPVs have large $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ of $0.7\substack{-1.0\mathrm{eV}}$ . It has been proposed that the lowest attainable $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ of OPVs is $0.6~\mathrm{{\eV}},$ , despite of several exceptional examples.17 As listed in Table 2, the $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ for the device of PTB7- Th:P-BNBP-Se and PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T are $0.56\mathrm{eV}$ and $0.47\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. To our best knowledge, the $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ of $0.47\ \mathrm{eV}$ is the lowest one for OPVs reported so far. Small $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ is always observed for all-PSCs with BNBP-based polymers as electron acceptors and the exact reason is unknown yet. We speculate that the small $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ is related to the high-lying LUMO levels of the BNBP-based polymers.
## Conclusions
In summary, we have developed a polymer acceptor based on BNBP unit and selenophene unit with optimal $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ to simultaneously enable charge separation and maximize $V_{\mathrm{oc}}.$ . BNBP-based polymers have delocalized LUMOs over the polymer backbones, so their $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ can be tuned by changing the comonomer unit. The $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ of P-BNBP-Se is lower by 0.16 eV than that of P-BNBP-T and consequently matches well with that of the polymer donor, PTB7-Th. While the all-PSC device based on PTB7-Th:P-BNBP-T shows a moderate PCE of $2.27\%,$ , the corresponding device with P-BNBP-Se as the acceptor exhibits a PCE as high as $4.26\%$ . Moreover, the device of PBNBP-Se shows a $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of up to $\boldsymbol{1.03\vee}$ and $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ as small as 0.56 eV. These results indicate that BNBP-based polymer acceptors have different electronic structures from those of classical NDIbased polymer acceptors and that they can give all-PSCs with remarkably high $\boldsymbol{V_{\mathrm{oc}}}$ and high PCE.
